WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.870
Last page, the lock manager handed out locks from its lock table.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:09.740
Now zoom all the way in, to a single lock.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.610
What does it look like from request to release, and what are the two modes it comes in?

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:16.711
Watch one lock through its whole life on row A.

00:00:16.711 --> 00:00:19.812
First, T1 requests the lock.

00:00:19.812 --> 00:00:22.912
The green flag is that request.

00:00:22.912 --> 00:00:26.013
The manager grants it.

00:00:26.013 --> 00:00:29.114
The red flag is the grant, and now T1 holds row A.

00:00:29.114 --> 00:00:32.215
From here, anyone else who wants the row waits.

00:00:32.215 --> 00:00:35.315
T2 requests the same lock and sits blocked the entire time.

00:00:35.315 --> 00:00:38.416
While it holds the lock, T1 does its work.

00:00:38.416 --> 00:00:41.517
It reeds row A, from reed start to reed end, then writes it.

00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:44.618
The hold covers both, real IO, not an instant.

00:00:44.618 --> 00:00:47.718
Finally, T1 releases the lock.

00:00:47.718 --> 00:00:50.819
The purple flag is that release.

00:00:50.819 --> 00:00:53.920
Only now does T2 get its turn.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.372
Not every lock has to block.

00:00:57.372 --> 00:01:00.824
The manager keeps two modes.

00:01:00.824 --> 00:01:04.276
First, the shared lock, S, for reading.

00:01:04.276 --> 00:01:07.728
Picture three fans checking seat A fifteen.

00:01:07.728 --> 00:01:11.180
Reeds never conflict, so they all take a shared lock and reed the seat at the same time.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.632
No one waits.

00:01:14.632 --> 00:01:18.084
Second, the exclusive lock, X, for writing.

00:01:18.084 --> 00:01:21.536
Now one fan buys seat A fifteen.

00:01:21.536 --> 00:01:24.988
A write must stand alone, so it takes an exclusive lock, writes sold, and everyone else is blocked until it releases.

00:01:24.988 --> 00:01:28.646
Put the rule in one table.

00:01:28.646 --> 00:01:32.305
The lock already held runs down the side, the lock requested across the top.

00:01:32.305 --> 00:01:35.963
Only one pairing is compatible.

00:01:35.963 --> 00:01:39.622
Shared on shared.

00:01:39.622 --> 00:01:43.280
Two reeds, no conflict, both proceed.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.939
Touch an exclusive lock, and you wait.

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:50.597
A held exclusive blocks every request, shared or exclusive alike, and a held shared blocks an incoming write.

00:01:50.597 --> 00:01:54.256
This is the conflict rule from last page, made operational.

00:01:54.256 --> 00:01:57.914
Same row, at least one write, you wait.

00:01:57.914 --> 00:02:02.357
So that is one lock.

00:02:02.357 --> 00:02:06.800
Request, grant, hold through a reed and a write, release.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.243
Two modes, shared for reeds, exclusive for writes, compatible only when both are shared.

00:02:11.243 --> 00:02:15.685
But a transaction holds many locks across its life, and granting each one correctly on its own still allows a broken interleaving.

00:02:15.685 --> 00:02:20.128
The discipline that closes that gap is Two-Phase Locking.

00:02:20.128 --> 00:02:24.571
That is next.
